these are the youngest journalists in  the city of firozabad they're working on  the next edition of their unique  magazine  balvani bacho kiawaz or the voice of  children is a magazine by and for young  people about child labour and their  rights  the stories that we write for the  magazine are all related to children and  inform readers about the reality of our  town when we share these stories with  government officials it helps them  understand the difficulties that  children face  fourteen-year-old harmony has been  contributing to the magazine for two  years she lives in an impoverished area  of firozabad where entire families are  employed in the glass bangles industry  she convinced one neighbor to stop her  three daughters aged between nine and  twelve from working and send them to  school instead  we have five children and no one to help  us so when we were in financial  difficulty we asked the children to work  a lot of children here work and at home  our children started helping us when we  were going through a rough patch  sarita devi sent her children back to  school when her husband recovered from  an illness and could work again but not  everyone would do the same  feruzabad's glass bangles industry is  200 years old and the factories are  known for their poor working conditions  generations of families have been  employed in the trade although many work  from home  when we walk through these narrow lanes  of firozabad we can see many children  making bangles rights groups say school  closures because of coronavirus and then  an outbreak of dengue fever mean more  children are forced to work because they  don't have access to online learning  this home children as young as six sit  in front of small flames using the heat  to join broken glass bangles they do  this for up to eight hours a day child  labor is illegal in india but rights  groups say it's difficult to stop those  working at home we had child labor 10  million in 2011 i'm doubling it only and  primarily because of covet as well so i  think what we can say coronavirus has  been impacting the world over but in  india the issues that we always had have  amplified to an extent which is actually  quite scary  the release of this year's voice of  children magazine has been delayed due  to lockdowns but harmony and her team  are now pushing the final touches on the  issue knowing their work has never been  more important  elizabeth puranam al jazeera ferozabad  uttar pradesh
 

